<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mellitus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/624">624</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mellitus was a member of the Gregorian mission sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 601, who became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 604 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Sæberht of Essex</a>. Expelled when Sæberht's pagan sons took power around 616, he later returned and was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 619 until his death in 624.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> described Mellitus as being of noble birth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> called him an abbot in his letters, but it is unclear whether he had been abbot of a Roman monastery or was given the rank to make him leader of the expedition. Nothing else of his background is known, though he was probably a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, like the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> consecrated by Augustine, the first Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>.</p><p>Gregory sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in June 601 in answer to Augustine's appeal for more clergy. The party carried books and objects needed for worship, and a letter urging King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a> to act like the Emperor Constantine I and to destroy pagan shrines. While Mellitus was on the road, Gregory sent a further letter allowing pagan temples to be converted into churches and animal sacrifices into Christian feasts. It is known as the Epistola ad Mellitum and was preserved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Augustine consecrated Mellitus as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the East Saxons in 604, making him the first Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> after the Roman departure. Mellitus had first baptised Sæberht, Æthelberht's nephew, who allowed the bishopric to be established. In February 610 Mellitus attended a council of bishops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> called by Pope Boniface IV, and brought papal letters back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He also joined Justus, the Bishop of Rochester, in signing a letter from Archbishop Laurence urging the Celtic bishops to adopt the Roman method of calculating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>.</p><p>After the deaths of Sæberht and Æthelberht, Sæberht's three pagan sons drove Mellitus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Bede says he was exiled for refusing them a taste of the sacramental bread. He took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with Justus, and Laurence recalled him after converting Æthelberht's successor Eadbald. He could not return to London, whose people remained pagan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mellitus succeeded Laurence as the third Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> in 619. He was said to have diverted a fire that threatened the church at Canterbury in 623, when he was carried into the flames and the wind changed direction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> praised his sane mind and noted that he suffered from gout. Mellitus died on 24 April 624 and was buried at St Augustine's Abbey that day.</p><p>He was revered as a saint, with 24 April as his feast day, and his shrine stood beside those of Augustine and Laurence at St Augustine's. After the Norman Conquest Goscelin wrote a life of him, and those with gout were urged to pray at his tomb.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mellitus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saeberht accepted Christianity around 604 and <b>Mellitus</b> became bishop of London, but the kingdom reverted to paganism after his death until Cedd's mission in the 650s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-essex">Kingdom of Essex</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Italian Peninsula, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42,14&amp;year=614" aria-label="Italian Peninsula on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4890 668 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mellitus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5340 938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5340 938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42,14&amp;year=614">What was here in 614 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
